--api-key: deny by default, allow the liveness paths.

The old guard authenticated only the /v1, /v2, /inference and /cohere
prefixes. /tokenize, /detokenize, /metrics, /docs and /server_info
answered without the key. /tokenize renders arbitrary text through the
model chat template, so this exposes the template, not only metadata.
The --api-key help text warned about this type of hole.

With --api-key set, every path now needs the bearer token, except
/health, /ping, /load and /version. Liveness probes and load balancers
need these four paths. A route that is added later is guarded by default.
It does not stay open until somebody extends a prefix list. A Prometheus
scraper that cannot send the key must use a separate listener. The
--api-key help text now describes the allowlist.

The allowlist is matched on the path without a trailing slash. This
middleware runs before the router, so /health/ does not get to the
redirect_slashes step of FastAPI. Without this, a liveness probe that
uses a trailing slash gets a 401.

Only a real CORS preflight skips the token check: an OPTIONS request with
an Origin header and an Access-Control-Request-Method header. This is the
test that Starlette's CORSMiddleware uses before it answers a preflight.
A bare OPTIONS request needs the token. /metrics is a mounted Prometheus
app and answers any method, so a bare OPTIONS /metrics returned the full
exposition without the key. Without Origin, CORSMiddleware sends the
request through to the app, so both headers are necessary.

On v0.30.0: authenticate.py is unchanged from v0.29.0 and the patch applies to it as cut; cli_args.py's help-text hunk lands 7 lines lower (#50195, #53353, #55176 moved it). The routes 0.30 adds (/v1/responses/render, the --enable-scale-out endpoints) fall under the deny-by-default rule with no allowlist change.

